from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('21');add=A.add
add('5.1-01','Rubbing suitable materials can transfer electrons between them.','An electroscope detects effects of charge redistribution through leaf or pointer deflection.','Charge is separated, not created from nothing. One body can gain electrons and become negative while the other loses electrons and becomes positive. A neutral electroscope can deflect when a charged object approaches because its charges redistribute; this does not by itself identify the approaching sign. A known initial charge can support a carefully interpreted sign comparison.','Explain how rubbing and an electroscope can demonstrate electrostatic charging.','Link rubbing to electron transfer and opposite net charges.~The charged object can redistribute charge in an electroscope and cause deflection.')
add('5.1-01-M01','Frictional charging involves electron transfer between suitable contacted materials.','The paired system retains its total charge if no charge enters or leaves elsewhere.','Rub a suitable dry plastic rod with cloth and test its interaction with small neutral paper pieces. Attraction demonstrates an electric effect but does not prove the paper initially has opposite net charge: polarisation can attract a neutral object. Avoid humid conditions that allow charge to leak away rapidly.','Describe a simple demonstration of frictional charging.','Rub a dry insulating rod with suitable cloth and show that it can attract small paper pieces.')
add('5.1-01-M02','An electroscope’s conducting parts allow mobile charge to redistribute.','Like charges on the movable leaf and stem can produce repulsion and deflection.','Bring a charged rod near the cap without contact and observe deflection. Remove it and note whether the initial state returns. Contact can leave net charge on the instrument, unlike simple temporary induction in an isolated neutral electroscope. Keep the instrument dry and discharge it between neutral-start trials.','Describe how an electroscope can detect a charged object nearby.','Bring the object near its cap and observe a reproducible leaf or pointer deflection due to charge redistribution.')
add('5.1-02','Electric charge has positive and negative signs.','A neutral object has balanced total positive and negative charge, not an absence of charged particles.','Protons carry positive charge and electrons negative charge. Ordinary charging changes the electron balance in this model; the positive nuclei remain within the material. Neutrality concerns the total, so a neutral conductor can still have locally separated induced charges.','State the two signs of electric charge.','Positive and negative.')
add('5.1-03','Electric charge is measured in coulombs.','The SI symbol for coulomb is C.','Charge is a quantity, while current is its flow rate. A transferred charge of 2 C is not a current of 2 A unless the time relation is specified. Keep the charge unit separate from the letter C sometimes used for heat capacity in a different topic.','State the SI unit of electric charge.','Coulomb, C.')
add('5.1-04','Like net charges repel and unlike charges attract.','A charged body can also attract a neutral polarisable body.','Repulsion between two tested objects is strong evidence that both have like charges in the simple electrostatic test. Attraction alone does not identify opposite signs because induced charge separation can bring the nearer opposite charge closer. Explain the relative distances when using this neutral-attraction mechanism.','State the interaction between two negative charges.','They repel one another.')
add('5.1-05','Electrostatic forces can position toner, remove airborne particles or steer charged ink droplets.','Each application requires charge and an electric field arranged for the desired motion.','A photocopier attracts toner to a patterned surface. A precipitator charges particles and collects them on suitable electrodes. A continuous ink-jet design can deflect charged droplets between plates. Not every printer uses the same mechanism, so the last example concerns that specified design.','Explain the shared electrostatic principle in a precipitator and a charged-droplet printer.','Link charged material to an electric force in a field.~The force directs particles to collectors or steers droplets to a chosen path.')
add('5.1-05-M01','Electrostatic photocopying uses a charge pattern to attract toner selectively.','The toner is then transferred and fixed to paper.','Illumination changes the charge pattern on a suitable drum so toner marks the intended regions. Transfer moves the pattern to the paper, and fixing makes it persist. The electric role is selective attraction, not merely warming every part of the sheet.','State the role of electrostatic attraction in photocopying.','It attracts toner selectively to a charge pattern.')
add('5.1-05-M02','An electrostatic precipitator charges suspended particles and attracts them to collecting surfaces.','This can remove particulate matter from a gas stream.','The gas flows through a controlled field region, particles acquire charge and then move toward a suitable electrode. Collected material must be removed so it does not simply accumulate indefinitely. This removes particles rather than guaranteeing removal of every gaseous pollutant.','State how an electrostatic precipitator removes particles.','It charges the particles and attracts them to collecting electrodes.')
add('5.1-05-M03','In a charged-droplet ink-jet system, an electric field deflects charged droplets.','Changing charge or the field changes the droplet path in the intended design.','Droplets enter a region between charged plates and experience a force depending on sign. Neutral droplets are not deflected by the same simple qE mechanism. The example concerns electrostatic steering rather than every possible commercial ink-jet mechanism.','State the electric-field role in a charged-droplet ink-jet printer.','It steers charged droplets toward selected positions.')
add('5.1-06','Lightning is a large electrical discharge associated with charge separation in clouds and their surroundings.','A sufficiently strong field can ionise air and create a conducting path.','Before the discharge, the air is a poor conductor under ordinary conditions. Once ionised, charge can move rapidly along a channel, transferring energy and heating the air. The resulting rapid expansion contributes to thunder. The event does not mean protons leave every object and fly upward as the usual charging mechanism.','Explain how a charged cloud can produce a lightning discharge.','Charge separation produces a strong electric field.~Air can ionise, forming a conducting path for rapid charge flow.')
add('5.1-07','An electric field is a region where a charge experiences an electric force due to its charge.','Field direction is defined by the force on a positive test charge.','The test charge should be small enough not to disturb the source arrangement appreciably. A negative charge experiences force opposite to the defined field direction. A field can exist before the test charge is introduced; the test reveals it rather than creating the source field.','Define an electric field.','It is a region in which a charge experiences a force due to its electric charge.')
add('5.1-08','Conductors have charges able to move through the material relatively readily.','In ordinary solid-metal conductors, mobile electrons carry charge; an insulator lacks comparable free movement.','Copper is a conductor; dry plastic and glass are typical insulators under ordinary conditions. An insulator can still polarise, meaning its charges shift slightly within bound structures. Insulation is conditional: a sufficiently strong electric field can cause breakdown. This does not remove the usefulness of the ordinary classification.','Explain a conductor-insulator difference using the simple electron model.','A metal conductor has mobile electrons able to move through it.~An ordinary insulator does not have comparable freely mobile electrons throughout its bulk.')
add('5.1-09','Electric field lines indicate the force direction on a positive test charge.','They point away from positive sources and toward negative sources.','A point source gives a radial pattern. Far from plate edges, oppositely charged parallel plates give an approximately uniform field with straight parallel equally spaced lines. Curving near edges is a limitation of the ideal central-region drawing. Lines do not cross because the field cannot have two directions at one point.','Explain how point-charge and parallel-plate field patterns differ.','Link a point charge to radial field lines.~The central region between large opposite parallel plates has approximately straight parallel equally spaced lines.')
add('5.1-09-M01','A positive point charge has radially outward electric field lines.','A negative point charge has the same radial geometry with inward arrowheads.','Draw several evenly distributed rays about the charge and label its sign. Line spacing increases farther away, representing weaker field in a qualitative diagram. Do not draw closed circles, which would indicate tangential rather than radial field direction.','Draw the electric field around an isolated positive point charge.','Draw radial lines spreading from the charge.~Put outward arrowheads on them to show force direction for a positive test charge.')
A.geometry('5.1-09-M01',[dict(points=[[.15*math.cos(t),.15*math.sin(t)],[2*math.cos(t),2*math.sin(t)]],arrow=True) for t in [i*math.pi/4 for i in range(8)]],labels=[[-.1,-.1,'+']],title='Positive point-charge field')
add('5.1-09-M02','The central field between opposite parallel plates is approximately uniform.','Its direction is from the positive plate toward the negative plate.','Draw equal-length parallel plates, label their signs and show straight parallel equally spaced lines between central regions. Use arrows across the gap rather than along the metal. Near edges the real field curves, so the simple construction is a central-region approximation.','Draw the central electric field between a positive plate on the left and a negative plate on the right.','Draw straight parallel equally spaced lines across the gap.~Arrowheads must point left to right, from positive toward negative.')
plates=[dict(points=[[x,-2],[x,2]],color='black') for x in [0,3]]+[dict(points=[[0,y],[3,y]],arrow=True) for y in [-1.5,-.5,.5,1.5]]
A.geometry('5.1-09-M02',plates,labels=[[-.4,0,'+'],[3.2,0,'−']],title='Parallel-plate field: central region')
add('5.1-09-M03','Field direction follows positive-test-charge force.','Electron force is opposite to the field direction.','For plates with positive on the left and negative on the right, the field arrow points right, while a free electron accelerates left if other forces are negligible. Do not reverse the definition merely because electron motion is being discussed.','Draw the electric-field arrow and electron-force arrow between a positive left plate and negative right plate.','Draw the field arrow toward the right, from positive to negative.~Draw the electron-force arrow toward the left because the electron is negatively charged.')
A.geometry('5.1-09-M03',[dict(points=[[0,-1],[0,1]],color='black'),dict(points=[[3,-1],[3,1]],color='black'),dict(points=[[.5,.4],[2.5,.4]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[2.5,-.4],[.5,-.4]],arrow=True,color='tab:red')],labels=[[-.3,0,'+'],[3.2,0,'−'],[1,.6,'Field'],[1,-.7,'Electron force']],title='Field direction differs from electron force')
add('5.1-10','Charging a conductor by induction uses an external field, charge redistribution and a controlled connection to Earth.','The order of removing the earth connection and inducing object determines the final state.','Bring a negative rod near an isolated conductor without touching. Electrons are repelled; connect the conductor to Earth so some electrons leave. Disconnect Earth while the rod remains, then remove the rod. The conductor is left positive and its charge redistributes. Removing the rod before disconnecting Earth can allow charge to return and defeats the intended result.','Explain how a negative rod can leave a conductor positively charged without touching it.','With the rod nearby, connect Earth so repelled electrons leave.~Disconnect Earth first and then remove the rod, leaving an electron deficit and positive net charge.')
A.misconception('MIS-17','Positive frictional charging ordinarily means loss of electrons.','The simple rubbing model does not transfer protons between the materials.','The nuclei remain bound in the materials while electrons are exchanged. Removing some negative charge leaves the pre-existing positive charge in excess. Describing the outcome as proton gain confuses a net charge imbalance with the mobile particle responsible.','A plastic sample becomes positive after rubbing. Explain why proton gain is not the correct ordinary charging account.','The error treats protons as the transferred charges in the rubbing model.~The sample instead lost electrons, leaving a positive net charge.')
C=A.choice
C('5.1-02','What signs of charge are used?',('Positive and negative','These are the two signs.'),('North and south','These name magnetic poles.'),('Hot and cold','These describe thermal comparisons.'),('Light and heavy','These describe different properties.'))
C('5.1-03','What unit measures charge?',('C','Coulomb is the charge unit.'),('A','Ampere measures current.'),('V','Volt measures energy per charge.'),('W','Watt measures power.'))
C('5.1-04','What do two negative charges do?',('Repel','Like signs repel.'),('Attract solely due to opposite signs','Their signs are the same.'),('Become magnetic north poles','Charge is not a magnetic pole.'),('Necessarily vanish','Repulsion does not destroy charge.'))
C('5.1-08','Which is a typical conductor?',('Copper','It has mobile electrons.'),('Dry plastic','It is an ordinary insulator.'),('Dry glass','It is ordinarily an insulator.'),('A vacuum as a metal wire','It is not a solid electron-conducting wire.'))
C('5.1-05-M02','What does a precipitator collect?',('Charged particles from a gas','An electric force moves them to collectors.'),('Uncharged pollutant gas molecules as its defining action','It does not guarantee complete gas-pollutant removal.'),('Only sound waves','Those are not charged particles.'),('Newly made protons','That is not its process.'))
C('5.1-07','Field direction is defined using which test charge?',('Positive','This is the direction convention.'),('Negative','Its force is opposite the defined direction.'),('Neutral only','It has no simple qE force.'),('No specified sign','A direction convention requires one.'))
C('5.1-01-M02','Can a neutral electroscope deflect when a charged rod approaches without contact?',('Yes, by redistribution','Its net charge can remain zero.'),('No, only contact has electric effects','Fields act without contact.'),('Only if protons leave the cap','Electrons redistribute in the metal.'),('Only because it becomes hot','The intended effect is electrostatic.'))
C('5.1-01','A material gains electrons. What net charge can result?',('Negative','Electrons add negative charge.'),('Positive from gaining negative charge','That reverses the sign.'),('Magnetic south','This is not a charge sign.'),('No possible charge change','Electron imbalance changes net charge.'))
C('5.1-04','A charged rod attracts neutral paper. What is supported?',('Polarisation can account for attraction','Opposite nearer induced charge can dominate.'),('The paper must already have opposite net charge','Attraction alone does not prove that.'),('The rod must be neutral','A charged rod can attract neutral material.'),('Charge has been created without transfer','The observation does not require that.'))
C('5.1-09-M03','A field points right. Which way is electric force on an electron?',('Left','Its charge is negative.'),('Right by the positive convention','That convention applies to positive test charge.'),('Zero due to electron charge','An electron is charged.'),('Out of the page necessarily','That is not set by this electric field.'))
C('5.1-10','With a negative rod nearby, an earthed conductor loses electrons. What must be disconnected first to retain the intended charge?',('Earth connection','Then remove the rod.'),('The rod’s influence first','Charge can return through Earth.'),('The conductor’s atoms','Atoms do not need removal.'),('Every electron','Only a small imbalance is required.'))
C('5.1-09-M02','Where is the simple uniform-plate-field model most appropriate?',('Near the central region between broad plates','Edge effects are smaller there.'),('Far outside the pair','The field pattern differs.'),('At every edge with no curvature','Real edge fields curve.'),('Inside a distant uncharged rod','That is another arrangement.'))
P=A.part
P('5.1-03','State the unit of charge.','Coulomb.',1,'One mark for coulomb or C.')
P('5.1-04','State the interaction between like charges.','Repulsion.',1,'One mark for repel.')
P('5.1-08','Explain the conducting behaviour of a metal.','It has mobile electrons that can move through the material and carry charge.',2,'One mark for mobile electrons; one for charge transport.')
P('5.1-01','Explain positive charging of a rubbed insulating sample in the simple model.','Electrons leave the sample. Its remaining positive and negative charges are unbalanced with a positive excess.',2,'One mark for electron loss; one for positive net imbalance.')
P('5.1-10','Explain why the earth connection is removed before the inducing rod.','It traps the intended net charge on the isolated conductor. If the rod is removed first, electrons can return through the earth connection and restore neutrality.',2,'One mark for isolating charge; one for explaining the alternative sequence.')
